WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.390
As it turns out, we didn't really say "bye
bye" to the cast of American Pie.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:09.194
Here's what they've all been up to since appearing
in the 1999 classic.

00:00:09.364 --> 00:00:15.450
Jason Biggs was the actor tasked with bringing
that infamous apple pie moment to life, and

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:21.080
we can only imagine he had a million questions
for his director before doing the deed.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.090
That's probably true of most of his scenes
in "American Pie" because more than any other

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.890
character, Jim suffers the brunt of the film's
angsty teen indignities.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:31.308
"Again!?"

00:00:31.308 --> 00:00:32.524
"Not again."

00:00:32.524 --> 00:00:34.365
"Not again, man!"

00:00:34.761 --> 00:00:36.010
"Is that possible?"

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:41.559
Whatever the case, one has to admire Biggs'
dedication to enduring those indignities,

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:46.829
and he leans so fully into every salacious
moment that you simply can't imagine "American

00:00:46.829 --> 00:00:47.879
Pie" without him.

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:53.280
While "American Pie" indeed made the relatively
unknown Biggs a star, he's had trouble replicating

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.710
such meteoric success in the years since.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:01.030
Still, the actor has made a mark in the entertainment
biz, carving out a career with films including

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:07.150
"Loser," "Saving Silverman," and "Who We Are
Now," as well as memorable work on Netflix's

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.500
prison dramedy "Orange is the New Black".

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.110
More recently, Biggs starred on two short-lived
series: the Fox family sitcom "Outmatched"

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.638
and the 2021 E! game show "Jason Biggs' Cash
at Your Door."

00:01:19.638 --> 00:01:24.990
While "American Pie" is essentially about
a bunch of horny dudes trying to score, it

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:31.390
was also one of the first "horny dudes" movies
that fully acknowledged that horny girls exist, too.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.130
And even if it clearly objectifies some of
its female characters, "American Pie" really

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:40.970
did better than most teen sex flicks in depicting
its female characters as fiercely intellectual

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.280
human beings with desires of their own.

00:01:43.522 --> 00:01:44.170
"I'm going to do it.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.420
I'm going to have sex."

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.420
"Now."

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.860
"And it's going to be right, and it's going
to be just perfect."

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.370
"Perfect sex."

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:49.946
"Thank you."

00:01:50.518 --> 00:01:51.518
"Please."

00:01:51.606 --> 00:01:56.680
Alyson Hannigan's flute-playing "band geek"
Michelle is one of the film's female characters

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.620
most distinctly in the middle of that equation,
mostly because "American Pie" doesn't initially

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.750
paint her as an object of desire.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:09.859
That quickly changes when she drops that shocking
line about the intimate nature of her relationship

00:02:09.859 --> 00:02:10.989
with her flute.

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:17.269
At that wholly unexpected turn, she actually
becomes the adorable, band-loving sexpot that

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:19.290
cinema didn't know it needed.

00:02:19.818 --> 00:02:20.629
"What's my name?

00:02:21.025 --> 00:02:22.145
Say my name, b---h!"

00:02:22.145 --> 00:02:23.409
"Michelle, Michelle!"

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:27.330
Of course, out of all the young stars in the
original "American Pie," Hannigan was likely

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.700
the one who needed little introduction to
the film's target audience because she'd already

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.529
made a splash on the small screen via "Buffy
the Vampire Slayer" as Willow Rosenberg.

00:02:36.529 --> 00:02:41.999
Of the film's cast, Hannigan is also the one
who scored the biggest post-"Pie" hit, joining

00:02:41.999 --> 00:02:47.429
another all-star comedy ensemble for the prime-time
juggernaut known as "How I Met Your Mother."

00:02:47.785 --> 00:02:52.211
Of all the young men who got their big break
in "American Pie," Chris Klein was the one

00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:54.420
most primed for superstardom.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.420
That's largely because he's got chiseled,
leading man looks, but it helps that he's

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:03.739
a better-than-average actor, has solid comedic
timing, and can even belt out a tune with

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:05.038
the best of them.

00:03:05.038 --> 00:03:07.062
“Yeah, magic.”

00:03:07.062 --> 00:03:13.349
And as it happens, 1999 was the year Klein
seemed most destined for fame, with winning

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:19.819
turns in both "American Pie" as Oz the hunky
but sensitive jock and in critical fave "Election"

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:22.620
as Paul the lovably dimwitted football player.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:28.120
However, "American Pie" was clearly the more
high-profile project, and Klein rode the initial

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.930
wave of stardom to a leading role in the sci-fi
action remake "Rollerball," as well as largely

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:37.459
under-seen supporting turns in "We Were Soldiers"
and "Just Friends".

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:41.689
Soon after that "Just Friends" appearance,
Chris Klein further lost his way in Hollywood,

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:46.140
spending much of the mid-2000s appearing in
forgettable films and series too numerous

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.140
to recount.

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.919
Klein finally started to turn things around
with a supporting turn performance opposite

00:03:50.919 --> 00:03:54.090
Elijah Wood in the gonzo FX dramedy "Wilfred."

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:59.010
Since then, he's gone on to deliver work in
the CW hit "The Flash", and on Netflix's warm

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.577
and fuzzy hit "Sweet Magnolias" as Dr. Bill
Townsend.

00:04:02.873 --> 00:04:10.689
Mena Suvari had a fantastic 1999 — and not
just because she played Michelle in "American Pie".

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:15.219
She also played Angela in future Best Picture
winner "American Beauty," which was ironically

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:23.100
released just after Suvari's other 1999 offering,
"American Virgin" — since renamed "Live Virgin".

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.770
Clearly, 1999 was an all-American year for
Mena Suvari at the cinemas.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:33.750
And as it happens, she played a virginal teen
in all three of those films, though most would

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.010
agree her Michelle in "American Pie" was the
most honorable.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:41.670
It was also the role that put the actor's
piercing vulnerability front and center and

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:47.380
allowed her to craft as earnest a portrait
of teenaged tenderness as the screen has seen.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.880
"Oz, I know."

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.820
In fact, Suvari has spent much of her career
since trying to shed that innocent persona,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:57.570
a task made more difficult given her own fragile
features.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.970
She's often succeeded in that endeavor, though,
with edgier turns in films like "Sugar &amp; Spice,"

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:03.610
"Edmond," and "Factory Girl."

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:08.060
While Suvari has some movies on the horizon,
she's appeared frequently on the small screen

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.820
over the years, playing in "Six Feet Under,"
"Chicago Fire," "American Woman," and "American

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:16.128
Horror Story" since her "American Pie" breakout.

00:05:16.528 --> 00:05:21.660
If Alyson Hannigan was the female cast member
of "American Pie" who needed little introduction

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.010
to fans, it's safe to say Thomas Ian Nicholas
was her male counterpart.

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.870
Of all the up-and-coming talent in "American
Pie," he was the only one who could count

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:34.940
himself a full-on child star, having already
spent a decade in front of the camera before

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:36.420
appearing as Kevin in the film.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.240
"No, that's great, man.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.240
That's awesome."

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:44.370
Nicholas' career pre-"Pie" is fairly impressive,
with the actor earning credits on "Who's the

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:49.390
Boss?", "Baywatch", and "Married... with Children"
before he was even 10 years old.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:54.350
He'd also scooped a big screen credit in "Radio
Flyer" before fronting "Rookie of the Year"

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:56.710
and Disney's "A Kid in King Arthur's Court."

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:02.370
As it was, "American Pie" was really the first
"adult" role in Thomas Ian Nicholas' career,

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:07.370
and the actor made the most of the moment,
crafting one of the more amiable horny screen

00:06:07.370 --> 00:06:09.090
teens in recent cinema.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:15.030
Once he got a taste of more adult fare, Nicholas
never looked back to his family-friendly beginnings.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:19.800
He scored complex roles in the likes of "The
Rules of Attraction," "Stealing Sinatra,"

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.960
and indies like "Please Give."

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:27.220
Most recently, he portrayed Walt Disney in
"Walt Before Mickey" and legendary director

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:31.754
Martin Scorsese in James Franco's star-studded
indie "Zeroville".

00:06:31.848 --> 00:06:37.670
Tara Reid's pre-"Pie" resume wasn't super
impressive, but she'd still scored scene-stealing

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:42.890
turns in "Urban Legend," "Cruel Intentions,"
and something like two scenes in the Coen

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.990
Brothers' immortal classic "The Big Lebowski."

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:46.680
"Brandt can't watch though.

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.706
Or else he has to pay a hundred."

00:06:49.706 --> 00:06:50.640
"That's marvelous."

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.690
Still, her work as Vicky displayed a depth
many hadn't seen from Reid before, one which

00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:00.570
both male and female fans of "American Pie"
could easily relate to.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:05.420
Reid seemed destined for stardom after the
film's release, and reinforced her name with

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.300
roles in underperforming flicks like "Dr.
T and the Women," "Josie and the Pussycats"

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.240
and "National Lampoon's Van Wilder."

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.750
And don't forget her funny arc on "Scrubs."

00:07:14.926 --> 00:07:16.256
"We'd still die."

00:07:16.256 --> 00:07:17.554
"We'd still what?"

00:07:17.598 --> 00:07:21.140
"If the elevator cable snapped, even if we
timed it perfectly and jumped up right before

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.470
impact, we'd still be crushed to death.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.168
I wish I'd have taken the stairs."

00:07:24.168 --> 00:07:29.950
However, like many of her "American Pie" co-stars,
Reid had some trouble in the mid-2000s.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:35.190
Nevertheless, she's become a regular fixture
of B-movies and schmaltzy Christmas flicks,

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:41.016
and made an appearance as herself in Amazon's
hyper-violent superhero satire "The Boys."

00:07:41.202 --> 00:07:47.810
Natasha Lyonne stole the show as the gloriously
quippy intellectual Jessica, projecting attitude,

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:50.988
wit, and genuine warmth well beyond her years.

00:07:50.988 --> 00:07:53.890
"Oh Kev, oh.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:54.890
Yeah.

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:55.890
Comprende?"

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:57.910
"You mean an orgasm?"

00:07:57.910 --> 00:07:59.160
"You got it, stud."

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:03.020
Of course, those who'd been tracking the actor's
career prior to "American Pie" were already

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:07.940
familiar with her sardonic charm, thanks largely
to her breakout turn the year prior in the

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.720
classic indie comedy "Slums of Beverly Hills."

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:14.730
However, things have been a bit complicated
in the years since "American Pie" for Lyonne,

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:18.730
with the actor's personal and professional
life seeing plenty of ups and downs.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:23.310
But even in spite of the struggles, Lyonne
is perhaps the "Pie" star with the highest

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:27.950
batting average when the camera's rolling,
stealing scenes in indie flicks such as "But

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:31.630
I'm a Cheerleader," "Die Mommy Die!", and
"Ad Astra".

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:36.840
She's also appeared in television series like
"New Girl," "Weeds", and all manner of projects

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:37.840
in between.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:43.530
Lyonne is probably best known for her heartbreaking
work on Netflix's prison dramedy "Orange is

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:48.180
the New Black" — a role that truly breathed
new life into her career.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:54.540
It came courtesy of a daring and skillful
performance in the serio-comic series "Russian Doll."

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:59.720
Did somebody say "actors who've seen their
share of ups and downs since their "American Pie" breakthrough?"

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:02.740
Because Sean William Scott has certainly seen
his.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:06.640
Scott made his big-screen debut in the film,
turning what might've been little more than

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:14.140
a supporting turn as the token "teen movie
jerk" into a career-defining triumph of cinematic smarm.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:17.333
"No way!"

00:09:17.333 --> 00:09:18.487
"Steve!

00:09:18.487 --> 00:09:20.760
You can at least look at me when you say that."

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.260
In Scott's hands, Stifler was a teen movie
jerk for the ages, with the actor indulging

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:31.750
in enough stilted attitude, boorish entitlement,
and vulgar language to make a mean girl blush.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:36.120
So good was Scott in the role — and in ensuing
sequels — that it's safe to assume he'll

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:41.234
never fully break free from Stifler's surprisingly
long shadow.

00:09:41.234 --> 00:09:43.006
"Can I get a hallelujah?"

00:09:43.006 --> 00:09:44.992
"Hallelujah, Stifler!"

00:09:44.992 --> 00:09:47.310
That hasn't stopped him from trying, however.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:52.110
The actor spent much of the 2000s on the verge
of superstardom, appearing in numerous classic

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.800
comedies such as "Old School", "Dude, Where's
My Car?" and "Road Trip".

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:01.150
But every time he seemed to take a step forward,
there was another "Bulletproof Monk" or "Mr.

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:03.380
Woodcock" to knock him back two.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:07.360
Personal demons eventually caught up with
Scott, however, and save for 2011's misfit

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.700
hockey marvel "Goon," the 2010s were a rough
stretch.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:15.170
He's been on the rebound, though, with a return
to the animated wilds of the "Ice Age" franchise,

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:19.970
a "Goon" sequel, and a starring role as a
small-town minister on the Fox comedy "Welcome

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:20.970
to Flatch."

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:25.440
While "American Pie" broke a little ground
in the teen sex comedy genre by giving the

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.940
ladies their due, it's still very much a film
focused on the male characters.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:35.100
That means there's a lot of objectification
going on, and Shannon Elizabeth's foreign

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.930
exchange student, Nadia, is ground zero for
most of it.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:43.740
So much so that she doesn't have much dialogue
in the film, and what she does have leans

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:46.370
heavily into the realm of male fantasy.

00:10:46.788 --> 00:10:48.360
"I could change clothes at your place."

00:10:50.361 --> 00:10:54.460
"Uh…yeah."

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:59.110
As it is, if "American Pie" were made today,
it's possible Nadia wouldn't even be in it.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:03.890
But she is, and if we're being completely
honest, Shannon Elizabeth does her best to

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:08.270
make her more of an actual human being than
the script ever bothers to.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:12.520
Unfortunately, not many filmmakers have seen
past her looks over the years, which means

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:15.910
there aren't many standout roles on her post-"Pie"
resume.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:21.880
She was, however, in Kevin Smith's "Jay and
Silent Bob Strike Back" and its recent sequel.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:26.400
She also scored a recurring role on "That
'70s Show", and she scored some laughs in

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.400
"Scary Movie."

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:32.593
Plus, she got to give Nadia some much-needed
refining in the 2012 misfire "American Reunion."

00:11:32.593 --> 00:11:37.790
Eddie Kaye Thomas showed legit comic chops
as Paul Finch, a.k.a.

00:11:37.790 --> 00:11:40.589
the wisdom-seeking know-it-all in "American
Pie."

00:11:40.589 --> 00:11:45.480
Thomas has more than kept himself busy in
the years since Finch infamously did the pool

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:49.880
table mambo with Stifler's mom in the waning
moments of the first film.

00:11:51.727 --> 00:11:54.390
"Women, like a fine wine, only get better
with age."

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:59.190
Like virtually all his co-stars, Thomas returned
for three "American Pie" sequels.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:03.510
He also landed a steady gig providing the
high-pitched voice of comically-troubled teen

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:08.720
Barry — as well as various minor characters
— in nearly 100 episodes of the long-running

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:10.680
animated comedy "American Dad!"

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.420
He featured prominently in the "Harold &amp; Kumar"
franchise as well.

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:18.700
But aside from those projects, there are a
few near misses spotting his resumé — such

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:22.070
as "Til Death" and Tom Green's "Freddy Got
Fingered".

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:28.250
Most famously, Kaye spent four years portraying
Dr. Toby Quinn-Curtis, a super-genius crime-solver,

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:30.402
on the CBS crime procedural "Scorpion.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:35.910
Older viewers who saw "American Pie" when
it hit theaters in 1999 were likely already

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:42.190
familiar with Eugene Levy, who portrayed Jim's
progressive and understanding-to-a-fault dad.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:46.514
"Well, we'll just tell your mother uh, that
we uh, that we ate it all."

00:12:46.514 --> 00:12:53.191
An influential, major comedy star in the 1970s
and early 1980s, Levy was an original, prominent

00:12:53.191 --> 00:12:58.410
cast member on various "SCTV" late-night sketch
shows produced in his native Canada.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:03.090
This earned him supporting roles in several
major '80s comedy movies, including "National

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:07.750
Lampoon's Vacation," "Splash," "Club Paradise,"
and "Armed and Dangerous."

00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:13.290
After acting steadily in numerous little-heralded
and little-watched projects, "American Pie"

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.010
proved to be a major comeback vehicle for
Levy.

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:20.890
He would go on to appear in all the official
"American Pie" sequels and even anchor three

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:25.529
direct-to-video raunchy comedies under the
"American Pie Presents" banner.

00:13:25.529 --> 00:13:30.420
Levy co-starred opposite Steve Martin in "Bringing
Down the House," and alongside Samuel L. Jackson

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:31.500
in "The Man."

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:36.270
And he was a pivotal part of Christopher Guest's
troupe of improv-adept comic actors in the

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:41.110
mockumentaries "Best in Show," "A Mighty Wind,"
and "For Your Consideration."

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:46.270
But Levy's most recent success overshadows
everything else, even "American Pie."

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:51.930
With his son, Dan Levy, he created the riches-to-rags
Canadian sitcom "Schitt's Creek."

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:56.940
He starred as Johnny Rose, a role that won
him an Emmy Award when "Schitt's Creek" swept

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.820
the comedy portion of the ceremony in 2020.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.510
Aside from the original "Harold &amp; Kumar" trailer,
there aren't many reminders that John Cho

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:07.060
was in "American Pie."

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:11.460
"Newline Cinema presents that Asian guy from
American Pie."

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:16.362
But whether you realize it or not, he's one
of the reasons you know what a MILF is.

00:14:16.362 --> 00:14:19.506
"M,I,L,F. Mom I'd like to f—-!"

00:14:19.506 --> 00:14:21.060
"Yeah dude!"

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:25.920
John Cho was indeed one of the so-called "MILF
guys" in "American Pie," though he also popped

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.450
up in a couple of "vocal jazz group" scenes.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:33.480
While Cho would go on to a bigger role and
actually be credited as John in subsequent

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:38.100
"American Pie" sequels, his "MILF" chanting
moment in the original flick is likely the

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:40.960
first time most moviegoers took note of the
actor.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:45.660
His career has shifted dramatically in the
years since, with Cho scoring a co-lead opposite

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:49.029
Kal Penn in the stoner cult-classic "Harold
&amp; Kumar" franchise.

00:14:49.029 --> 00:14:54.130
He is also featured prominently in the recent
"Star Trek" movies and landed charming one-off

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:58.020
turns in TV hits like "New Girl," and "How
I Met Your Mother."

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:01.870
Most recently, however, Cho has made a name
for himself on the indie scene via lauded

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:05.390
films like "Columbus," "Gemini," and "Searching."

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:09.810
He also played the iconic Spike Spiegel in
the short-lived live-action reboot of the

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.676
anime classic "Cowboy Bebop".

00:15:11.676 --> 00:15:17.480
When she appeared as an object of lust and
desire — aka, Stifler's Mom — in "American

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:21.200
Pie," Jennifer Coolidge wasn't well known
outside of comedy circles.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:25.680
She'd appeared on the short-lived mid-'90s
network sketch comedy show "She TV" and had

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.260
popped up in "Seinfeld" and "A Night at the
Roxbury."

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:29.700
"What's up?"

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:30.612
"Not much.

00:15:30.612 --> 00:15:32.942
Just giving you an eighty dollar ticket."

00:15:33.096 --> 00:15:35.754
"She is so into you."

00:15:35.754 --> 00:15:40.980
But "American Pie," along with Christopher
Guest's 2000 semi-improvised mockumentary

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:45.110
"Best in Show," would provide Coolidge back-to-back
career breaks.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.710
Showcasing her skill at playing kooks, weirdos,
and ditzes.

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:54.279
You most likely remember her role as the friendly
manicurist and confidante Paulette in both

00:15:54.279 --> 00:15:56.640
popular "Legally Blonde" movies.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.160
She eventually re-teamed with Guest for "A
Mighty Wind" and "For Your Consideration,"

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:05.330
while also reprising her role as Stifler's
Mom in the "American Pie" sequels.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:09.870
She portrayed Joey's agent on the "Friends"
spinoff "Joey," joined the cast of "2 Broke

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:15.580
Girls," and earned rave reviews for her more
dramatic work on the HBO vacation-from-hell

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:17.372
miniseries "The White Lotus."

